; =============================================================================
; Tunah tuner -- automatic vectorization
; =============================================================================
; Purpose       : Hold the vector form of a computation next to its scalar form
;                in the same e-graph, and form a vector store out of two
;                adjacent stores to consecutive addresses. The vector rules
;                give the extractor the cheaper spelling of a vectorized loop
;                body; the superword rule is the one place where this tuner
;                creates a vector node from scalar input.
; Stage         : Instruction level machine IL, after loop normalization and
;                before the vector instruction forms are selected.
; Vector vocabulary, all elementwise and all of the same length:
;   av-add av-sub av-mul av-and av-or av-xor av-shl av-neg av-cmp av-select
;   av-sext av-zext av-interleave av-reduce-add av-broadcast av-load av-store
;   superword: av-store-pair p a b denotes two adjacent byte stores of a and
;   b at p and at p plus 1, which is one vector store of two byte lanes.
;   The rules below use a subset of this vocabulary. The rest is declared so
;   that a vector term produced by the vectorizer can be ingested into a
;   session that has this file loaded, without the adapter having to special
;   case an operator that only appears in the input.
; Preconditions :
;   * Every av- operator is elementwise and the operands are vectors of equal
;     length, an invariant the vectorizer establishes before ingestion. A
;     mismatch is an adapter error and must be rejected, not rewritten.
;   * An integer literal inside an av- pattern is a broadcast lane, so the
;     literal zero is a vector of zeros. That is what makes the annihilator
;     and neutral element rules below sound.
;   * Lanes are integers, so addition is associative and commutative and a
;     reduction distributes over an elementwise sum. The rules would not be
;     sound for floating point lanes, where reassociation changes the result.
;   * Distributing sign extension over arithmetic requires a host analysis
;     proving that every narrow signed lane result fits without overflow.
;     The vector-signed-*-fits predicates carry this proof obligation.
;   * av-broadcast takes a scalar and av-reduce-add returns a scalar. Neither
;     may be equated with a vector operand or an elementwise vector result.
;   * The superword rule requires the two stores to be adjacent in the same
;     block and to write consecutive byte locations, which is exactly what
;     the pattern states. A store to an address that is not a constant offset
;     from the other is not matched, so no aliasing argument is needed.
; Cost model    : A vector application costs 1 + arity regardless of its lane
;                count, so the vector spelling of a loop body is much cheaper
;                than the scalar one and the extractor prefers it. The
;                superword rule replaces two stores with one three-argument
;                store, which is also strictly cheaper, and it is the one rule
;                here that creates a node; it is the point of the tuner.
; Termination   : No rule takes a vector node apart and no rule matches on a
;                vector node in the left hand side of a forming rule, so the
;                vector forms are the leaves of this set. The superword rule
;                fires at most once per adjacent store pair and cannot reapply
;                to its own output. The set is monotone.
;
; Operator signatures required by this file:
; signatures: av-add 2, av-sub 2, av-mul 2, av-and 2, av-or 2, av-xor 2,
;             av-shl 2, av-neg 1, av-cmp 3, av-select 3, av-sext 1, av-zext 1,
;             av-interleave 2, av-reduce-add 1, av-broadcast 1, av-load 1,
;             av-store 2, av-store-pair 3, iadd 2, store8 2, seq 2
; =============================================================================

; A zero broadcast is the neutral element of an elementwise sum, so the add is
; the vector it started from.
(rule av-add-zero (av-add ?a 0) ?a)
; A one broadcast is the neutral element of an elementwise product.
(rule av-add-mul-one (av-mul ?a 1) ?a)
; A zero broadcast annihilates an elementwise product, and the result is the
; zero vector, which is the zero broadcast.
(rule av-mul-zero (av-mul ?a 0) 0)
; Every bit is its own exclusive or, so a vector exclusive or with itself is
; the zero vector.
(rule av-xor-self (av-xor ?a ?a) 0)
; A vector is its own and.
(rule av-and-self (av-and ?a ?a) ?a)
; A vector is its own or.
(rule av-or-self (av-or ?a ?a) ?a)
; A vector and the negation of itself sum to the zero vector.
(rule av-add-negation (av-add ?a (av-neg ?a)) 0)
; A shift by a zero count broadcast is the identity.
(rule av-shl-zero (av-shl ?a 0) ?a)
; A negation of the zero vector is the zero vector.
(rule av-neg-zero (av-neg 0) 0)
; Adding scalar zero before broadcasting does not change the broadcast lanes.
(rule av-broadcast-drop-zero-add (av-broadcast (iadd ?a 0)) (av-broadcast ?a))
; A lane-wise signed sum can be widened distributively only if no lane wraps.
(rule av-sext-of-add (av-sext (av-add ?a ?b)) (av-add (av-sext ?a) (av-sext ?b))
  :where (vector-signed-add-fits ?a ?b))
; The same proof obligation for every exact signed lane difference.
(rule av-sext-of-sub (av-sext (av-sub ?a ?b)) (av-sub (av-sext ?a) (av-sext ?b))
  :where (vector-signed-sub-fits ?a ?b))
; The same proof obligation for every exact signed lane product.
(rule av-sext-of-mul (av-sext (av-mul ?a ?b)) (av-mul (av-sext ?a) (av-sext ?b))
  :where (vector-signed-mul-fits ?a ?b))
; There is deliberately no rule distributing av-zext. A zero extension of a sum
; is not the sum of the zero extensions when the narrow sum overflows a lane,
; because the carry out of the narrow add is dropped by the widening and kept by
; the wide add, and no range or overflow analysis reaches this tuner. That is
; also why av-zext appears in the vocabulary below but in no rule: it is the
; spelling a vector term arrives in, not a distribution this file may assume.
; Interleaving commutes with an elementwise operation, because interleaving is
; a permutation of the lanes and a permutation of the inputs of a lane wise
; function is the lane wise function of the permuted inputs.
(rule av-interleave-of-add (av-interleave (av-add ?a ?b) (av-add ?c ?d))
  (av-add (av-interleave ?a ?c) (av-interleave ?b ?d)))
; A reduction distributes over an elementwise sum, because the lanes are
; integers and their total is the sum of the totals.
(rule av-reduce-of-add (av-reduce-add (av-add ?a ?b))
  (iadd (av-reduce-add ?a) (av-reduce-add ?b)))
; Two adjacent stores to consecutive byte locations are one vector store of two
; lanes. This is the superword level parallelism case, and the only rule in this
; directory that builds a vector node out of scalar nodes.
(rule av-store-pair (seq (store8 ?p ?a) (store8 (iadd ?p 1) ?b)) (av-store-pair ?p ?a ?b))
